Based on the visual analysis of the chest X-ray, the two small hyperintensities (white spots) indicated by the red boxes in the lower lung zones are located symmetrically overlying the breast tissue. Their position and appearance are characteristic of **nipple shadows**.

In medical imaging, particularly in frontal chest radiographs, nipple shadows are a common normal variant that can mimic pulmonary nodules. They are typically identified by their location (overlying the 4th to 6th ribs), symmetry, and lack of associated lung pathology. To confirm this, one might look for corresponding shadows on a lateral view or use nipple markers during subsequent imaging, but based on this single image, they represent normal anatomical structures.

{"answer": "Nipple shadows", "reason": "The two small hyperintensities are symmetrically located over the breast tissue and represent normal nipple shadows, which are a common finding on chest X-rays."}

